{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Moses brought their cause near before YHWH.</b> Some have explained that since the daughters of Zelophehad revealed in their plea that their father was not among the company of Moses' haters, this created a closeness of favor toward Moses in his own mind, and had he judged their case it would have been as though he accepted verbal bribery; therefore he held himself back from judgment and did not wish to judge them. But this does not seem correct, for if so Moses should have handed the case over to one of the judges of Israel — the officers of thousands and the officers of hundreds who were there — since he himself could not judge them." "<b>Rather,</b> the correct and true view is the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory, that the law was hidden from him and he did not know it at all. And because it is not a profound matter of law but rather one of the simple ones, dependent on reasoning (סברא) to the point that the nations judge by way of reason (sekhel) in such cases, therefore it was reckoned to him as a punishment — for its concealment from him was not on account of its depth but rather on account of punishment, as He said (Deuteronomy 1): ״והדבר אשר יקשה מכם תקריבון אלי״ — 'and the matter that is too hard for you, you shall bring near to me'; and therefore ״ויקרב משה את משפטן״ — 'and Moses brought their cause near.' And the reason the letter נו\"ן appears as a large letter is to hint at a sage who is great in Torah; and the Torah taught proper conduct (דרך ארץ), that a person should not render judgment in the presence of one who is greater than he, but rather he must bring the case before him — and so Moses did, for he would not judge the case of the daughters of Zelophehad until he asked the Holy One, blessed be He. And if you say: is it possible that he did not know? — it is possible concerning one of whom it is written (Numbers 12) ״בכל ביתי נאמן הוא״, who was expert in all the hidden secrets of the Torah and the mysteries of wisdom, how is it possible that he would not know this easy law? Rather, he did not wish to judge until he asked, in order to teach an a fortiori inference (ק\"ו) for the generations." "<b>Rather,</b> the view of our Rabbis of blessed memory is that the law was hidden from him and he did not know it at all. It is possible to say that the letter נו\"ן serves as a sign and allusion to the Gate of Fifty (שער החמשים), that he did not know it at all and it was not delivered to him, for it is 'before YHWH' (לפני ה') in the manner of the subtle inner essence (פנימיות) that is ungraspable; and this is what it says: ״את משפטן לפני ה'״ — for this law was hidden from him like the נו\"ן that is before YHWH — that is, the uppermost gate, the Gate of Fifty — even though the reason for the concealment is not the same in both cases, for the one is by way of punishment and the other by way of profundity." "<b>And they said</b> in the Midrash: the daughters of Zelophehad came to ask the law first from the officers of tens; the officers of tens asked the officers of fifties, in order to show them honor; the officers of fifties likewise showed honor to the officers of hundreds and asked them; the officers of hundreds likewise showed honor to the officers of thousands and asked them; the officers of thousands showed honor to Moses; Moses saw this and showed honor to the Shekhinah — and this is ״ויקרב משה את משפטן לפני ה'״ — 'and Moses brought their cause near before YHWH.' And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: this law was worthy of being written through Moses, but the daughters of Zelophehad merited, and it was written through them."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:36:34.153618749Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}